WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:07.860
- Hello. Hello, everyone.

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:09.810
I am Kaveh Khoshnood.

00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:12.280
I'm gonna start with a brief introduction

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:16.560
before I pass it over to my
colleague Dr. Anees Chagpar

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:18.720
for her presentation.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.930
I am a faculty at the Yale
School of Public Health,

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:26.110
and I am the faculty director
for Humanitarian Research Lab

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:30.210
with a particular interest
to address the health

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:33.510
of populations that are
affected by humanitarian crisis,

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:35.490
including armed conflict.

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:37.770
I've had a focus on the MENA region,

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:40.380
the Middle East, North Africa region.

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:44.310
My own training is in
infection disease epidemiology,

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:49.310
but every time I speak with
humanitarian organizations,

00:00:49.763 --> 00:00:53.370
the NCDs, noncommunicable
diseases, come up,

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:55.590
and cancer, cancer care and treatment,

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:57.400
has come up multiple times

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:03.320
when I have visited
these humanitarian NGOs.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:07.320
And I recall, it was kind
of a disturbing story.

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:10.020
When I visited Lebanon,

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:13.350
and I went to a humanitarian organization.

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:15.060
There was a Syrian oncologist

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:17.790
who had been displaced into Lebanon,

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:22.790
and he has started the mammography clinic

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:24.240
for Syrian refugees.

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:26.130
I think he was the only one doing that,

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:30.753
and he been screening
women for breast cancer.

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:34.230
He told me that one of these women had

00:01:34.230 --> 00:01:35.730
early-stage breast cancer,

00:01:35.730 --> 00:01:39.420
and they referred her to a UN agencies

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:44.070
that provide financial
support for cancer treatment,

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:46.807
and they told this woman that,

00:01:46.807 --> 00:01:49.470
"You're very early-stage breast cancer.

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:51.750
We cannot fund you.

00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:56.167
Come back," whatever year later or so.

00:01:56.167 --> 00:01:58.260
"If your breast cancer is advanced,

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:01.980
then we can provide support for you."

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:05.700
That was such a disturbing
story that I heard.

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:10.200
It's completely sort of
anti-public health issue

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:12.270
because we're supposed
to be able to screen

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:15.660
and treat breast cancer
patient as early as possible,

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:20.660
not wait, but these UN agencies
have very limited funds.

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:23.637
They make these decision about people

00:02:23.637 --> 00:02:26.220
who are in late-stage breast cancer,

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:30.390
are likely to die, they
kind of focus on them

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:31.920
the limited resources that they have.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:33.300
So I was very disturbed by that,

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.040
and I was trying to
figure out what I can do

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:37.800
to support this project.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:39.990
I ended up working with one

00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:43.920
of our students long ago named Jude Alawa

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:47.340
who comes from a Syrian family background.

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:50.790
He ended up doing the first study looking

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:55.320
at cancer awareness
and access to treatment

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:58.320
for cancer patients in Lebanon,

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:00.900
both in the Syrian refugee population

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:03.480
and the Lebanese population.

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:06.990
That was a very sort of
meaningful study that he did,

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:10.950
and it triggered conversation
about different stakeholders

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:12.423
to continue this work.

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:18.210
I then learned that our
colleagues called SAMS,

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:20.850
Syrian American Medical Society,

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:23.490
had launched a clinic,

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:27.900
an oncology clinic in
2018 in northwest Syria,

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:30.810
which, as you may know,
it's a conflict zone.

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:34.110
Northwest Syria is not
under government control,

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:37.100
and SAMS, Syrian American Medical Society,

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:39.870
is one of the few organizations

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:43.230
that was able to provide
care and treatment,

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:46.650
and they actually had set up a clinic

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:48.990
to take care of cancer patients.

00:03:48.990 --> 00:03:51.450
So I reached out to
them to see what support

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:54.930
we can provide to them,
and what they wanted was

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:56.880
they had been collecting a lot of data,

00:03:56.880 --> 00:03:58.380
but they really hadn't had the time

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:00.990
and capacity to finalize this data.

00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:04.650
They wanted us to support
then and put out reports.

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:07.413
I reached out to my
colleague Dr. Anees Chagpar,

00:04:08.359 --> 00:04:12.630
who's a wonderful surgeon,

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:16.050
oncologist, and I asked
whether she would be interested

00:04:16.050 --> 00:04:18.090
in participating in this project.

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:20.880
She said yes, she applied for the grant,

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:23.280
it's from the MacMillan Center,

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:25.440
and was able to launch the study

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:27.450
that she's gonna tell you about.

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:30.030
Just briefly, I'm gonna mention to you,

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:32.580
Dr. Anees Chagpar is a full professor

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:35.790
in the Department of Surgery
at the Yale School of Medicine.

00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:39.210
She has specific expertise
in breast cancer.

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:41.850
As you can see, she has
multiple master degree

00:04:41.850 --> 00:04:43.800
in addition to her medical degree.

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:45.420
She has a masters in public health.

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:49.380
She has a masters in bioethics
and medical humanities.

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:51.603
She has an MBA from Yale.

00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:56.613
And she's also a fantastic communicator.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:00.540
You may have come across
her Coursera course

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:03.753
on breast cancer, which
is extremely popular.

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:07.230
She's quite active on social media,

00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:10.020
and she's very passionate
about global health

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:12.783
and leadership and academic training.

00:05:14.652 --> 00:05:18.900
I'm really delighted that she
agreed to lead this project,

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:21.370
and today she's gonna present to you

00:05:22.522 --> 00:05:25.713
where this project has gone
over the last two years.

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:29.040
Dr. Chagpar, I'll pass it on to you.

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:29.883
Thank you so much.

00:05:29.883 --> 00:05:31.830
- Thank you so much, Kaveh.

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:35.130
So it's a real pleasure to be here

00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:37.710
to present this work.

00:05:37.710 --> 00:05:42.710
This, as Kaveh mentioned,
was a collaborative effort

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:46.770
between the School of Medicine,
the School of Public Health,

00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:51.030
as well as our colleagues
from around the world

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:54.123
through the Syrian
American Medical Society.

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:58.500
It was really a
mixed-methods investigation

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:01.530
into the experiences
and outcomes of patients

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:04.410
with breast cancer in the SAMS clinic

00:06:04.410 --> 00:06:06.483
in northwest Syria.

00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:11.370
So before we dive in,
here are my disclosures.

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:15.360
The ones at the top really
will not be relevant

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:16.680
to today's discussion.

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:19.050
I'm not going to be discussing anything

00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:23.550
that is pertinent to
those funding sources,

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:26.700
but I do want to highlight,
as Kaveh mentioned,

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:30.600
that the work that I'll
be presenting was funded

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:34.260
in part by the Yale MacMillan
Center, Program on Refugees,

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:37.260
Forced Displacement, and
Humanitarian Response,

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:41.433
and we are very grateful for that support.

00:06:42.570 --> 00:06:44.550
So if we take a step back

00:06:44.550 --> 00:06:46.740
and we think about the Syrian conflict,

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:50.010
this has really been going on since 2011,

00:06:50.010 --> 00:06:53.010
and it started as a
civilian uprising rooted

00:06:53.010 --> 00:06:54.213
in the Arab Spring.

00:06:55.290 --> 00:07:00.290
We know that this really
took on an armed conflict

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:03.540
and involved targeted strikes,

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:07.230
particularly on healthcare
personnel and facilities.

00:07:07.230 --> 00:07:10.050
It caused over 400,000 deaths,

00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:14.597
and there was more than
5.6 million refugees

00:07:14.597 --> 00:07:18.003
out of the pre-war
population of 22 million.

00:07:18.930 --> 00:07:23.010
That 5.6 million is
likely an underestimate.

00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:26.313
And World Health Organization, in 2017,

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:31.260
called the Syrian conflict
"the worst man-made disaster

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:34.557
the world has ever seen
since World War II."

00:07:35.910 --> 00:07:39.030
The chart which I show you below

00:07:39.030 --> 00:07:40.840
is really the change

00:07:43.503 --> 00:07:47.340
in level of life satisfaction
in 163 countries,

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:53.040
blue being better life satisfaction,

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:54.330
red being worse.

00:07:54.330 --> 00:07:55.950
And so you can see

00:07:55.950 --> 00:07:59.553
that over this period of time,

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:04.893
life satisfaction in Syria
has declined significantly.

00:08:07.290 --> 00:08:09.750
If we think about breast cancer in Syria,

00:08:09.750 --> 00:08:14.373
in particular, these are
data from Global Can,

00:08:15.660 --> 00:08:17.700
breast cancer, as many of you know,

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:19.540
remains the leading cause

00:08:21.029 --> 00:08:24.240
of cancer in women worldwide,

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:27.480
and that is certainly the case in Syria.

00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:30.690
It's also the leading cause of mortality.

00:08:30.690 --> 00:08:35.070
And here you can see that
this is not just among women,

00:08:35.070 --> 00:08:37.650
but breast cancer in Syria

00:08:37.650 --> 00:08:41.910
is the leading malignancy,
period, across all genders

00:08:41.910 --> 00:08:46.350
and the leading cause of
cancer-related mortality

00:08:46.350 --> 00:08:47.973
across all genders.

00:08:49.740 --> 00:08:52.740
So if we think about the map of Syria,

00:08:52.740 --> 00:08:56.310
I highlighted here the capital,

00:08:56.310 --> 00:08:59.490
Damascus, in red, which
you can see in the south,

00:08:59.490 --> 00:09:04.290
Aleppo is a large city in the north,

00:09:04.290 --> 00:09:06.360
and it was these two centers

00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:10.770
that really had a lot of the healthcare,

00:09:10.770 --> 00:09:14.340
especially prior to the conflict.

00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:16.630
Where SAMS came in was

00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:20.840
in establishing a clinic here in Idlib,

00:09:20.840 --> 00:09:23.640
in the northwestern part of Syria,

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:25.920
where, as Kaveh mentioned,

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:29.763
this was really a conflict area.

00:09:32.370 --> 00:09:34.860
And so the objectives of this study were

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:37.290
to understand the patient experience

00:09:37.290 --> 00:09:41.760
and pathways to diagnosis of
patients with breast cancer

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:45.810
at the SAMS-supported
facilities in northwest Syria

00:09:45.810 --> 00:09:49.320
and to evaluate factors
affecting stage at presentation

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:50.820
and outcomes of these patients.

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:53.880
So we did this with
both a qualitative study

00:09:53.880 --> 00:09:55.740
as well as a quantitative one,

00:09:55.740 --> 00:10:00.240
and I wanna walk you through
both parts of the study.

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:05.240
So for the qualitative study,
we set inclusion criteria

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:08.430
of patients who were
diagnosed with breast cancer

00:10:08.430 --> 00:10:12.750
at the SAMS facilities who
were living in northwest Syria

00:10:12.750 --> 00:10:15.900
who are at least 18 years of age or older.

00:10:15.900 --> 00:10:20.253
90 patients were screened,
and 65 patients were excluded.

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:24.840
The reasons for the exclusions
were that 40 of them

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:28.263
did not have a working
telephone number or WhatsApp,

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:32.850
25 had either died or declined due

00:10:32.850 --> 00:10:36.840
to current illness or lack
of incentive to participate,

00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:40.410
and three could not find
a time to be interviewed

00:10:40.410 --> 00:10:42.840
within the time period allotted.

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:45.780
So we were left with 22 patients

00:10:45.780 --> 00:10:48.810
who were interviewed between August

00:10:48.810 --> 00:10:51.243
and September of last year.

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:56.250
Informed consent was
taken by all participants.

00:10:56.250 --> 00:10:58.080
This was a study that was approved

00:10:58.080 --> 00:11:01.920
by the Yale Human
Investigations Committee.

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:06.150
The interviews were conducted
in conversational Arabic

00:11:06.150 --> 00:11:09.933
in the local dialect, and
they were done on WhatsApp.

00:11:10.890 --> 00:11:15.210
They were recorded,
transcribed, coded for themes.

00:11:15.210 --> 00:11:17.760
NVivo software was used.

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:21.810
And they looked at a
framework analysis method

00:11:21.810 --> 00:11:23.913
with a feminist lens.

00:11:24.900 --> 00:11:27.810
Here are the demographics
of the participants.

00:11:27.810 --> 00:11:32.810
So the mean age was 45
with a range of 24 to 67.

00:11:33.390 --> 00:11:36.390
Roughly half, a little
less than half, 41%,

00:11:36.390 --> 00:11:38.760
were internally displaced.

00:11:38.760 --> 00:11:43.170
You can see that there were
no patients in this study,

00:11:43.170 --> 00:11:47.910
where stage was known, who
had early-stage disease.

00:11:47.910 --> 00:11:49.230
All of these patients had

00:11:49.230 --> 00:11:52.080
either Stage II, III, or IV disease,

00:11:52.080 --> 00:11:56.493
the majority of whom, over
50%, were Stage II and III.

00:11:58.410 --> 00:12:01.230
18% were not educated.

00:12:01.230 --> 00:12:05.010
Only 14% had a university education.

00:12:05.010 --> 00:12:09.930
The majority of these women
had children and were married.

00:12:09.930 --> 00:12:13.410
And the majority of them were working

00:12:13.410 --> 00:12:16.893
as a housewife or were retired.

00:12:17.850 --> 00:12:19.380
So that gives you a sense

00:12:19.380 --> 00:12:22.923
of the population who were interviewed.

00:12:24.030 --> 00:12:26.640
And as these interviews were conducted,

00:12:26.640 --> 00:12:30.480
there were a number of
themes that emerged.

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:34.950
One was really this whole
idea of the challenges

00:12:34.950 --> 00:12:37.653
in terms of accessing oncology care,

00:12:38.850 --> 00:12:43.020
and many of the participants
were very grateful

00:12:43.020 --> 00:12:47.670
to SAMS for having
established oncology care

00:12:47.670 --> 00:12:49.890
in northwest Syria.

00:12:49.890 --> 00:12:52.620
They mentioned things like the fact

00:12:52.620 --> 00:12:54.450
that there was a center there,

00:12:54.450 --> 00:12:57.270
actually reduced some of the challenges,

00:12:57.270 --> 00:13:01.023
including cost of transport
and services in Turkey,

00:13:02.130 --> 00:13:04.860
being away from family
and feeling isolated

00:13:04.860 --> 00:13:08.370
because of the linguistic
barrier in Turkey,

00:13:08.370 --> 00:13:10.680
delays in permission to cross the border,

00:13:10.680 --> 00:13:12.660
and restrictions on companions.

00:13:12.660 --> 00:13:15.990
Remember that many of these patients,

00:13:15.990 --> 00:13:19.020
if they couldn't get care in Syria,

00:13:19.020 --> 00:13:22.533
Turkey would be their alternative.

00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:26.220
But that was not all roses.

00:13:26.220 --> 00:13:27.960
There were transportation issues

00:13:27.960 --> 00:13:31.500
within Syria too that were problematic.

00:13:31.500 --> 00:13:34.650
Another issue that came up was
really the financial strain,

00:13:34.650 --> 00:13:37.110
and I wanted to highlight
some of the quotes

00:13:37.110 --> 00:13:41.130
that we got from participants
who were interviewed.

00:13:41.130 --> 00:13:44.737
So participant number
eight, for example, said,

00:13:44.737 --> 00:13:46.500
"We struggled a lot

00:13:46.500 --> 00:13:49.770
because my husband doesn't
have a regular income.

00:13:49.770 --> 00:13:52.530
One day he works and another he doesn't.

00:13:52.530 --> 00:13:54.390
We are staying in tents,

00:13:54.390 --> 00:13:57.030
and now because of the chemotherapy,

00:13:57.030 --> 00:13:58.890
I feel too hot in the tent.

00:13:58.890 --> 00:14:01.380
However, I try not to complain.

00:14:01.380 --> 00:14:04.020
The living conditions are very difficult.

00:14:04.020 --> 00:14:07.080
No one helps us with
the cost of treatment.

00:14:07.080 --> 00:14:09.810
We had a freezer and refrigerator,

00:14:09.810 --> 00:14:12.867
but we sold them so I can
continue my treatment."

00:14:13.800 --> 00:14:17.460
And that gives you a
sense of what life is like

00:14:17.460 --> 00:14:21.210
for many of these people living in Syria

00:14:21.210 --> 00:14:26.070
in this conflict area and
the struggles that they face

00:14:26.070 --> 00:14:27.900
in terms of the day-to-day living

00:14:27.900 --> 00:14:31.053
as well as in affording
the cost of treatment.

00:14:32.820 --> 00:14:35.193
There were also delays in care.

00:14:36.690 --> 00:14:39.060
One of the things that I
should point out here is

00:14:39.060 --> 00:14:43.830
that radiation therapy is not
available in northwest Syria,

00:14:43.830 --> 00:14:47.010
and it's not available at
the SAMS Oncology Center

00:14:47.010 --> 00:14:49.230
in northwest Syria either.

00:14:49.230 --> 00:14:53.280
So for people who need radiation therapy,

00:14:53.280 --> 00:14:56.370
the only option is to go to Turkey.

00:14:56.370 --> 00:15:00.097
So participant number
one, for example, said,

00:15:00.097 --> 00:15:01.410
"There is only one doctor

00:15:01.410 --> 00:15:04.530
to see almost 200 patients a day

00:15:04.530 --> 00:15:06.840
at the hospital that I was referred to."

00:15:06.840 --> 00:15:08.670
She's talking here about the hospital

00:15:08.670 --> 00:15:10.420
that she was referred to in Turkey.

00:15:11.610 --> 00:15:15.750
She goes on to say, "One time
I stayed two months in Turkey

00:15:15.750 --> 00:15:19.680
until my turn arrived, and by
that time, the doctor said,

00:15:19.680 --> 00:15:23.130
'You will needs another
radiotherapy treatment.'"

00:15:23.130 --> 00:15:25.260
And this was a recurring theme,

00:15:25.260 --> 00:15:29.310
this idea of delays in
care being problematic

00:15:29.310 --> 00:15:30.933
for these patients.

00:15:32.700 --> 00:15:37.410
Another theme that came
out that was interesting

00:15:37.410 --> 00:15:39.783
was in human relationships.

00:15:40.860 --> 00:15:43.770
A couple of the participants noted

00:15:43.770 --> 00:15:47.520
the real positive aspects

00:15:47.520 --> 00:15:50.440
of their family
relationships that came out

00:15:51.360 --> 00:15:53.400
during their cancer journey.

00:15:53.400 --> 00:15:56.977
So participant number
13, for example, said,

00:15:56.977 --> 00:15:59.190
"My husband did the impossible

00:15:59.190 --> 00:16:02.760
and he left the family and
went with me to Turkey.

00:16:02.760 --> 00:16:04.650
He never left me alone.

00:16:04.650 --> 00:16:08.670
The amount of support I
got from him made me feel

00:16:08.670 --> 00:16:11.040
that there is someone who really believes

00:16:11.040 --> 00:16:13.380
that I can recover and survive.

00:16:13.380 --> 00:16:15.237
This had an important role."

00:16:16.350 --> 00:16:20.400
Participant number 17
had a similar experience.

00:16:20.400 --> 00:16:23.550
She says, "My parents-in-law supported me,

00:16:23.550 --> 00:16:25.770
and my husband, in particular,

00:16:25.770 --> 00:16:29.040
helped me in Turkey as my
parents are not around.

00:16:29.040 --> 00:16:32.580
He was the first one to
support me, thank God.

00:16:32.580 --> 00:16:34.710
The emotional and psychological part

00:16:34.710 --> 00:16:36.870
of this disease is important.

00:16:36.870 --> 00:16:40.710
As long as you have someone
next to you supporting you,

00:16:40.710 --> 00:16:42.870
you will forget about the disease,

00:16:42.870 --> 00:16:46.377
and he was there next
to me all the the time."

00:16:47.400 --> 00:16:50.580
And so we can see that for some patients,

00:16:50.580 --> 00:16:54.150
they had incredibly strong family support,

00:16:54.150 --> 00:16:55.350
which really helped them

00:16:55.350 --> 00:16:58.170
to get through the breast cancer journey.

00:16:58.170 --> 00:17:00.270
But it wasn't all good.

00:17:00.270 --> 00:17:04.927
So participant 18, for example, said,

00:17:04.927 --> 00:17:07.680
"Cancer wasn't as shocking for me

00:17:07.680 --> 00:17:10.380
as when my husband left me.

00:17:10.380 --> 00:17:14.310
You feel you are not useful
for anything anymore.

00:17:14.310 --> 00:17:18.237
This is harder than the
disease, cancer, itself."

00:17:19.110 --> 00:17:20.850
And he wasn't the only one.

00:17:20.850 --> 00:17:23.437
So participant number four said,

00:17:23.437 --> 00:17:25.500
"I have three children.

00:17:25.500 --> 00:17:28.590
In the beginning, my
sister took care of my son

00:17:28.590 --> 00:17:30.420
who was seven months old,

00:17:30.420 --> 00:17:33.663
and the other children were
taken care of by my parents.

00:17:34.590 --> 00:17:37.710
Then the community started
saying my husband needs

00:17:37.710 --> 00:17:39.900
to marry again and have another wife

00:17:39.900 --> 00:17:41.940
to take care of the children.

00:17:41.940 --> 00:17:44.820
He did this and took the children,

00:17:44.820 --> 00:17:47.670
so they are far away from me now."

00:17:47.670 --> 00:17:51.900
And so you can see it's
not just the cancer journey

00:17:51.900 --> 00:17:55.080
that many of these
patients had to deal with.

00:17:55.080 --> 00:17:58.020
It was also some of the
human relationships,

00:17:58.020 --> 00:18:03.020
the cultural relationships,
the family relationships

00:18:03.180 --> 00:18:05.403
that they also had to contend with.

00:18:07.920 --> 00:18:11.040
So that gave us a little bit of a taste

00:18:11.040 --> 00:18:15.810
of what the experience
of these patients were

00:18:15.810 --> 00:18:19.323
as they went through the
cancer journey in Syria.

00:18:20.430 --> 00:18:24.000
The second part of this study
was really quantitative,

00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:25.980
trying to get at,

00:18:25.980 --> 00:18:29.670
what is the presentation
clinically of these patients,

00:18:29.670 --> 00:18:33.510
how does that compare to
what we see here in the US,

00:18:33.510 --> 00:18:36.810
and what factors really impact stage

00:18:36.810 --> 00:18:39.090
at diagnosis, et cetera?

00:18:39.090 --> 00:18:43.080
So in order to answer the
quantitative part of this project,

00:18:43.080 --> 00:18:45.960
we review charts of
women who were diagnosed

00:18:45.960 --> 00:18:49.890
with breast cancer at the
SAMS Oncology Center in Idlib

00:18:49.890 --> 00:18:53.820
between 2018 and 2021.

00:18:53.820 --> 00:18:56.640
So we looked at 192 patients.

00:18:56.640 --> 00:19:00.720
The median age at diagnosis was 46

00:19:00.720 --> 00:19:03.840
with a range between 22 and 80.

00:19:03.840 --> 00:19:06.900
So just to put that into context, right,

00:19:06.900 --> 00:19:10.380
if you think about the age at diagnosis

00:19:10.380 --> 00:19:12.450
of breast cancer here in the US,

00:19:12.450 --> 00:19:13.920
these are data that I'm showing you

00:19:13.920 --> 00:19:16.260
from the American Cancer Society,

00:19:16.260 --> 00:19:20.490
you can see that the median
age of diagnosis here is 62,

00:19:20.490 --> 00:19:24.780
so a lot younger in terms of time,

00:19:24.780 --> 00:19:28.203
date at diagnosis for patients in Syria.

00:19:29.790 --> 00:19:33.570
Clinically, 16% of
these patients presented

00:19:33.570 --> 00:19:36.690
with clinical distant metastatic disease.

00:19:36.690 --> 00:19:39.180
70% had palpable lymph nodes

00:19:39.180 --> 00:19:41.430
at the time of their presentation.

00:19:41.430 --> 00:19:45.660
And at the time of surgery, 76% were found

00:19:45.660 --> 00:19:49.980
to have pathologically-involved
lymph nodes.

00:19:49.980 --> 00:19:52.260
And the median number of lymph nodes

00:19:52.260 --> 00:19:55.680
that were found to be positive was four.

00:19:55.680 --> 00:20:00.240
The invasive tumor size median

00:20:00.240 --> 00:20:01.800
was four centimeters,

00:20:01.800 --> 00:20:06.060
the range from 0.5 to 18 centimeters.

00:20:06.060 --> 00:20:09.480
So for those of you who
may not be as familiar

00:20:09.480 --> 00:20:13.290
with breast cancer and stage in the US,

00:20:13.290 --> 00:20:15.090
just to put this into context,

00:20:15.090 --> 00:20:18.030
and here, again, I'm showing
you the data from the ACS,

00:20:18.030 --> 00:20:22.500
you can see here in the light pink bars,

00:20:22.500 --> 00:20:27.270
across different races, distant
metastatic disease ranging

00:20:27.270 --> 00:20:31.110
anywhere between 5 and 8%.

00:20:31.110 --> 00:20:33.690
And you see that in Syria,

00:20:33.690 --> 00:20:36.390
the rate of having
distant metastatic disease

00:20:36.390 --> 00:20:38.430
was roughly double that.

00:20:38.430 --> 00:20:40.500
And so we know that that

00:20:40.500 --> 00:20:43.830
is certainly significant
prognosis of the patients

00:20:43.830 --> 00:20:47.370
who have distant metastatic
disease is far inferior,

00:20:47.370 --> 00:20:51.003
and, in fact, when people have
distant metastatic disease,

00:20:52.020 --> 00:20:56.373
although the disease may be
treatable, it is not curable.

00:20:57.750 --> 00:21:01.080
You can see in the medium pink bars

00:21:01.080 --> 00:21:05.460
anywhere between 24 and 31%

00:21:05.460 --> 00:21:08.280
of patients in the US will present

00:21:08.280 --> 00:21:10.260
with what's called regional disease,

00:21:10.260 --> 00:21:12.780
so lymph node-positive disease.

00:21:12.780 --> 00:21:17.780
In Syria, again, more
than twice that at 76%.

00:21:18.120 --> 00:21:21.000
And even when we have
patients here in the US

00:21:21.000 --> 00:21:24.300
who have no positive disease, oftentimes,

00:21:24.300 --> 00:21:28.530
that node positivity is
only one or two lymph nodes,

00:21:28.530 --> 00:21:31.950
whereas in Syria, the median number

00:21:31.950 --> 00:21:34.890
of lymph nodes involved was four.

00:21:34.890 --> 00:21:38.430
In the US, many patients are presenting

00:21:38.430 --> 00:21:41.520
with even pre-invasive cancers,

00:21:41.520 --> 00:21:44.400
DCIS or Stage I.

00:21:44.400 --> 00:21:48.030
Remember back to the qualitative
portion of the study,

00:21:48.030 --> 00:21:52.020
where none of those patients
had that early-stage disease.

00:21:52.020 --> 00:21:55.590
That's also reflected in
this quantitative analysis,

00:21:55.590 --> 00:21:57.960
where the median size
was four centimeters.

00:21:57.960 --> 00:22:01.392
So we're thinking more into that Stage II

00:22:01.392 --> 00:22:03.603
to III breast cancer.

00:22:06.270 --> 00:22:08.640
In terms of histology,

00:22:08.640 --> 00:22:12.990
so of the 192 charts that we reviewed

00:22:12.990 --> 00:22:16.650
where we had information about histology,

00:22:16.650 --> 00:22:20.984
only three patients,
1.6%, present with DCIS.

00:22:20.984 --> 00:22:24.720
DCIS is pre-invasive cancer,

00:22:24.720 --> 00:22:26.640
essentially curable.

00:22:26.640 --> 00:22:30.743
In the US, that number is about 17%.

00:22:31.950 --> 00:22:35.220
In Syria, of the invasive breast cancers,

00:22:35.220 --> 00:22:37.590
roughly 81% were invasive ductals.

00:22:37.590 --> 00:22:41.400
13% were invasive lobular.

00:22:41.400 --> 00:22:45.123
Those are similar to what
we see here in the US.

00:22:46.440 --> 00:22:50.100
They had about 12% who
were triple negative.

00:22:50.100 --> 00:22:52.410
That is to say that their tumors

00:22:52.410 --> 00:22:55.320
did not express ER, PR,

00:22:55.320 --> 00:22:59.670
or HER2 receptors on their surface.

00:22:59.670 --> 00:23:02.640
The number in the US is roughly similar,

00:23:02.640 --> 00:23:05.393
slightly lower, at 10%.

00:23:09.090 --> 00:23:10.740
When we looked at how these patients

00:23:10.740 --> 00:23:12.930
were diagnosed and treated,

00:23:12.930 --> 00:23:17.880
you can see that 92%
had the diagnosis made

00:23:17.880 --> 00:23:19.830
based on excision.

00:23:19.830 --> 00:23:23.070
There's been now an
international consensus

00:23:23.070 --> 00:23:27.180
that ideally we want to
diagnose these patients

00:23:27.180 --> 00:23:30.120
using minimally-invasive techniques,

00:23:30.120 --> 00:23:34.350
like needle biopsies,
usually a core needle biopsy.

00:23:34.350 --> 00:23:39.350
And in Syria, only 4% of
the patients were diagnosed

00:23:39.360 --> 00:23:41.550
based on core needle biopsy.

00:23:41.550 --> 00:23:46.550
Now our analysis did not
get into why that was.

00:23:46.650 --> 00:23:49.770
I can tell you based on experiences

00:23:49.770 --> 00:23:54.330
that we've had in other
areas of the world,

00:23:54.330 --> 00:23:58.140
for example, my brother does
a lot of work in global health

00:23:58.140 --> 00:24:02.760
in Tajikistan, where core needle biopsies,

00:24:02.760 --> 00:24:05.520
the actual device itself,

00:24:05.520 --> 00:24:07.890
are just very hard to find.

00:24:07.890 --> 00:24:10.590
There's just simply a lack of supplies.

00:24:10.590 --> 00:24:13.560
And so that may have played
a role here, but, again,

00:24:13.560 --> 00:24:16.470
we don't have information on that.

00:24:16.470 --> 00:24:19.620
Despite these cancers being fairly large

00:24:19.620 --> 00:24:22.890
and locally advanced at presentation,

00:24:22.890 --> 00:24:25.890
where we here in the
West would have treated

00:24:25.890 --> 00:24:28.740
those patients with chemotherapy first,

00:24:28.740 --> 00:24:32.220
many of those patients
went on to receive surgery

00:24:32.220 --> 00:24:35.580
as their initial treatment, 94% of them.

00:24:35.580 --> 00:24:38.700
And again, further work needs to occur

00:24:38.700 --> 00:24:41.760
to really dig into why that was.

00:24:41.760 --> 00:24:44.610
It may have been the fact

00:24:44.610 --> 00:24:47.580
that chemotherapy may
not have been available,

00:24:47.580 --> 00:24:52.080
it may have been due to anxiety
on part of the patients,

00:24:52.080 --> 00:24:56.850
or the fact that they were
facing fungating breast lesions,

00:24:56.850 --> 00:24:59.580
where what we would call here

00:24:59.580 --> 00:25:03.330
in the West a toilet mastectomy

00:25:03.330 --> 00:25:06.750
or a palliative mastectomy, was required

00:25:06.750 --> 00:25:10.563
simply to control the local disease.

00:25:11.550 --> 00:25:14.190
Again, we do not have information

00:25:14.190 --> 00:25:17.763
on the rationale behind these figure,

00:25:18.600 --> 00:25:22.320
but rather this is more
hypothesis generating

00:25:22.320 --> 00:25:25.140
rather than definitive.

00:25:25.140 --> 00:25:29.940
What we do know is that they
had a very respectable time

00:25:29.940 --> 00:25:32.310
between diagnosis and surgery.

00:25:32.310 --> 00:25:35.910
A median of 15 days is
certainly acceptable

00:25:35.910 --> 00:25:37.560
even here in the West,

00:25:37.560 --> 00:25:41.040
but you can see that
that range was from zero,

00:25:41.040 --> 00:25:44.970
perhaps by people who had
an excision of their cancer,

00:25:44.970 --> 00:25:48.060
which was also curative at the same time,

00:25:48.060 --> 00:25:51.630
all the way up to 756 days,

00:25:51.630 --> 00:25:56.430
so that is more than two years,

00:25:56.430 --> 00:25:58.593
which certainly is problematic.

00:26:00.630 --> 00:26:03.690
We then looked at a number of
other interesting questions

00:26:03.690 --> 00:26:05.850
that we were able to parse out

00:26:05.850 --> 00:26:09.300
from the data that we collected.

00:26:09.300 --> 00:26:11.880
One was exposure to chemical weapons.

00:26:11.880 --> 00:26:15.990
Now in the analysis, in the
data that we had gathered,

00:26:15.990 --> 00:26:19.770
only seven patients had
exposure to chemical weapons,

00:26:19.770 --> 00:26:22.410
and so that's something
certainly to keep in mind.

00:26:22.410 --> 00:26:26.010
These are small numbers,
and so, as you can see,

00:26:26.010 --> 00:26:30.420
we did not achieve statistical
significance on the basis

00:26:30.420 --> 00:26:33.390
of any of these clinical
pathologic factors,

00:26:33.390 --> 00:26:35.580
but it may well have
been related to the fact

00:26:35.580 --> 00:26:38.820
that we were comparing to
such a small sample size.

00:26:38.820 --> 00:26:42.690
But if you look at the
actual numbers themselves,

00:26:42.690 --> 00:26:45.270
you can see that those

00:26:45.270 --> 00:26:47.850
who did have exposure to chemical weapons,

00:26:47.850 --> 00:26:51.630
albeit that these are not
statistically significantly,

00:26:51.630 --> 00:26:56.340
tended to be higher grade,
more often triple negative,

00:26:56.340 --> 00:26:59.550
more often to have clinical
distant metastasis,

00:26:59.550 --> 00:27:01.860
more often to be node positive,

00:27:01.860 --> 00:27:05.430
more often to be larger in size,

00:27:05.430 --> 00:27:09.660
which raises the question,
if we expanded this study,

00:27:09.660 --> 00:27:13.620
wouldn't we in fact reach
statistical significance?

00:27:13.620 --> 00:27:16.710
Again, hypothesis generating

00:27:16.710 --> 00:27:19.023
rather than definitive.

00:27:21.240 --> 00:27:23.550
The next question that we looked at was

00:27:23.550 --> 00:27:28.170
seeing the difference between
citizens or host populations

00:27:28.170 --> 00:27:30.180
and those who were internally displaced.

00:27:30.180 --> 00:27:31.650
So here you can see

00:27:31.650 --> 00:27:35.880
that in the cohort
charts that we looked at,

00:27:35.880 --> 00:27:40.880
over half of these patients
were in fact displaced.

00:27:41.130 --> 00:27:43.800
This goes back to the fact

00:27:43.800 --> 00:27:46.890
that in the northwest Syria,

00:27:46.890 --> 00:27:51.180
that is roughly the percentage

00:27:51.180 --> 00:27:54.570
of the population who are displaced.

00:27:54.570 --> 00:27:56.190
And you can see here that we found

00:27:56.190 --> 00:27:58.980
no statistically significantly difference

00:27:58.980 --> 00:28:02.373
in term of any of these
clinical pathologic factors,

00:28:03.210 --> 00:28:06.570
which goes to say that people are people.

00:28:06.570 --> 00:28:09.720
Whether you were displaced
or not displaced,

00:28:09.720 --> 00:28:11.820
the type of breast cancer that we saw

00:28:11.820 --> 00:28:15.180
amongst these two populations
were very similar.

00:28:15.180 --> 00:28:18.147
You can look at the
individual parameters here,

00:28:18.147 --> 00:28:20.853
and you can see that they're very similar.

00:28:22.110 --> 00:28:25.920
One of the things that
was interesting, however,

00:28:25.920 --> 00:28:28.500
when we looked at the differences

00:28:28.500 --> 00:28:30.840
between the host population

00:28:30.840 --> 00:28:33.810
and the internally displaced people

00:28:33.810 --> 00:28:35.730
was that there was a trend,

00:28:35.730 --> 00:28:38.580
albeit not a statistically
significant trend,

00:28:38.580 --> 00:28:42.390
but still a trend with
the p-value of 0.051,

00:28:42.390 --> 00:28:46.170
towards a shorter time from
diagnosis to the initiation

00:28:46.170 --> 00:28:49.530
of treatment for internally
displaced people.

00:28:49.530 --> 00:28:53.910
Now on the face of it, that
may seem counterintuitive.

00:28:53.910 --> 00:28:57.450
You would think that those
who are internally displaced

00:28:57.450 --> 00:29:00.390
would have a harder time accessing care,

00:29:00.390 --> 00:29:03.333
they should have a
longer time to treatment,

00:29:04.560 --> 00:29:07.020
but I think that there are a few things

00:29:07.020 --> 00:29:08.310
to keep in mind here.

00:29:08.310 --> 00:29:11.220
One is that, again, remember,

00:29:11.220 --> 00:29:14.820
this may not truly be a
shorter time interval.

00:29:14.820 --> 00:29:18.720
We did not hit statistical significance,

00:29:18.720 --> 00:29:20.220
it's only a trend,

00:29:20.220 --> 00:29:23.940
and it is still a relatively
small sample size.

00:29:23.940 --> 00:29:25.590
However, the other thing that I think

00:29:25.590 --> 00:29:27.600
that is important to keep in mind is

00:29:27.600 --> 00:29:31.890
that these patients had
already accessed care.

00:29:31.890 --> 00:29:35.280
They had already been
diagnosed with breast cancer

00:29:35.280 --> 00:29:37.710
at the SAMS facility.

00:29:37.710 --> 00:29:40.980
And so the ray of light here might be

00:29:40.980 --> 00:29:42.960
that those people who were diagnosed

00:29:42.960 --> 00:29:46.170
at the SAMS facility
in northwestern Syria,

00:29:46.170 --> 00:29:49.350
it didn't seem to really impede

00:29:49.350 --> 00:29:51.390
those who were internally displaced

00:29:51.390 --> 00:29:53.280
in terms of getting their treatment.

00:29:53.280 --> 00:29:57.090
If anything, their treatment
was slightly shorter,

00:29:57.090 --> 00:30:01.320
14 days versus 19 days
from the time of diagnosis

00:30:01.320 --> 00:30:04.650
than the host population, and
this may have been related

00:30:04.650 --> 00:30:06.660
to other issues, right?

00:30:06.660 --> 00:30:10.470
The host population might
have included citizens

00:30:10.470 --> 00:30:14.940
who were employed, who might
have had other obligations

00:30:14.940 --> 00:30:17.220
where it may have delayed them somewhat

00:30:17.220 --> 00:30:19.173
in terms of getting their treatment.

00:30:20.250 --> 00:30:24.320
Clinically, the difference
between 19 days and 14 days,

00:30:24.320 --> 00:30:27.630
it is not terribly dramatic,

00:30:27.630 --> 00:30:30.150
and so think it's at least reassuring

00:30:30.150 --> 00:30:32.940
that internally displaced people,

00:30:32.940 --> 00:30:37.890
once the diagnosis is
made, could get a treatment

00:30:37.890 --> 00:30:40.743
in a relatively expeditious fashion.

00:30:42.780 --> 00:30:47.010
So in summary, there are a number

00:30:47.010 --> 00:30:50.190
of unique challenges faced
by breast cancer survivors

00:30:50.190 --> 00:30:54.150
in northwestern Syria in
terms of access to care,

00:30:54.150 --> 00:30:58.980
transportation, financial
strain, family tensions.

00:30:58.980 --> 00:31:02.730
And when we look at
breast cancer in Syria,

00:31:02.730 --> 00:31:05.910
at least in the
northwestern part of Syria,

00:31:05.910 --> 00:31:08.523
in the SAMS Oncology Centers there,

00:31:10.380 --> 00:31:15.270
the presentation tends to be younger women

00:31:15.270 --> 00:31:19.590
at a higher stage, more
often triple negative,

00:31:19.590 --> 00:31:22.080
more often diagnosed with excision

00:31:22.080 --> 00:31:24.603
than what we would see here in the US.

00:31:25.590 --> 00:31:29.760
Again, this study only
scratches the surface.

00:31:29.760 --> 00:31:33.093
I think that there is still
plenty of work to be done.

00:31:34.320 --> 00:31:37.710
If we compare those to who were exposed

00:31:37.710 --> 00:31:40.200
to chemical weapons versus not,

00:31:40.200 --> 00:31:43.050
there were no statistically
significant differences

00:31:43.050 --> 00:31:45.930
in terms of clinical pathologic factors,

00:31:45.930 --> 00:31:48.060
but there, again, I wonder whether that is

00:31:48.060 --> 00:31:50.640
just related to the small sample size,

00:31:50.640 --> 00:31:53.910
because when we look
at the actual numbers,

00:31:53.910 --> 00:31:58.410
we do see differences
that may be meaningful.

00:31:58.410 --> 00:32:00.990
But citizen or host populations

00:32:00.990 --> 00:32:03.390
and those who were internally displaced

00:32:03.390 --> 00:32:08.190
had very similar clinical
pathologic characteristics,

00:32:08.190 --> 00:32:10.650
and at least for those who were treated

00:32:10.650 --> 00:32:14.010
in the SAMS facility in
Idlib, it was no difference

00:32:14.010 --> 00:32:17.730
in the time to treatment
between those populations.

00:32:17.730 --> 00:32:19.710
If anything, there was a trend

00:32:19.710 --> 00:32:22.800
to the internally displaced
population getting

00:32:22.800 --> 00:32:25.470
a slightly shorter time interval

00:32:25.470 --> 00:32:27.363
between diagnosis and treatment.

00:32:29.220 --> 00:32:31.290
So this study would not have been possible

00:32:31.290 --> 00:32:34.470
without a wealth of collaborators.

00:32:34.470 --> 00:32:38.220
Kaveh mentioned how
this study got started.

00:32:38.220 --> 00:32:42.510
Jude Alawa, who Kaveh mentioned,

00:32:42.510 --> 00:32:43.830
should be on this slide,

00:32:43.830 --> 00:32:48.030
so my apologies for that omission.

00:32:48.030 --> 00:32:51.420
You can see here our SAMS colleagues

00:32:51.420 --> 00:32:53.790
as well as a number of students at Yale

00:32:53.790 --> 00:32:57.780
who participated in
making this work possible.

00:32:57.780 --> 00:33:02.520
So I'm gonna stop there,
and I am happy to answer

00:33:02.520 --> 00:33:04.653
any questions that you might have.

00:33:05.490 --> 00:33:10.110
- Hi, I just have a general question.

00:33:10.110 --> 00:33:14.613
So you mentioned about
the difference between,

00:33:16.080 --> 00:33:20.670
no difference between cancer treatment

00:33:20.670 --> 00:33:25.670
for people who are

00:33:25.770 --> 00:33:30.090
internally displaced and
their host community.

00:33:30.090 --> 00:33:31.360
I don't know if

00:33:33.930 --> 00:33:35.580
with this SAMS,

00:33:35.580 --> 00:33:39.570
do they collect also
information for other diseases?

00:33:39.570 --> 00:33:43.650
Is that like something
that you see as well?

00:33:43.650 --> 00:33:46.860
- Yeah, so our project only focused

00:33:46.860 --> 00:33:50.610
on breast cancer patients, so
the charts that we reviewed

00:33:50.610 --> 00:33:53.370
were all breast cancer patients.

00:33:53.370 --> 00:33:55.860
I do know that the SAMS facility,

00:33:55.860 --> 00:34:00.860
there does treat other
kinds of cancers and may,

00:34:01.200 --> 00:34:02.700
although I'm not sure, and, Kaveh,

00:34:02.700 --> 00:34:04.590
you might know better than me,

00:34:04.590 --> 00:34:09.590
may also treat other diseases as well.

00:34:10.020 --> 00:34:13.173
I know that they do treat other cancers,

00:34:14.100 --> 00:34:15.870
but we did not look at that.

00:34:15.870 --> 00:34:18.843
That might be something
for future study as well.

00:34:20.370 --> 00:34:21.720
- Maybe I can ask a question

00:34:21.720 --> 00:34:24.630
till there's a processing, I'm sure.

00:34:24.630 --> 00:34:26.490
Thank you so much for that presentation,

00:34:26.490 --> 00:34:29.610
and it's really, to my knowledge,

00:34:29.610 --> 00:34:33.100
Syrian American Medical
Society's one of the few

00:34:34.980 --> 00:34:37.350
international humanitarian organizations

00:34:37.350 --> 00:34:40.230
that actually offers
cancer care and treatment.

00:34:40.230 --> 00:34:42.750
I think there's a lot of
humanitarian organizations

00:34:42.750 --> 00:34:45.090
that, for a variety of reasons,

00:34:45.090 --> 00:34:48.150
don't see cancer care treatment as part

00:34:48.150 --> 00:34:52.440
of their responsibilities.

00:34:52.440 --> 00:34:55.470
I think Doctors Without
Borders, MSF does that.

00:34:55.470 --> 00:34:57.770
So, of course, I'm
interested in figuring out,

00:34:59.285 --> 00:35:00.660
how do we make this case

00:35:00.660 --> 00:35:03.177
to other humanitarian
organizations that cancer care

00:35:03.177 --> 00:35:06.300
and treatment is not something
that should be neglected?

00:35:06.300 --> 00:35:09.723
Because the population
you're talking about,

00:35:11.458 --> 00:35:14.760
they're stuck for decades.

00:35:14.760 --> 00:35:18.030
So they don't just have
some acute, let's say,

00:35:18.030 --> 00:35:19.170
infectious disease issues.

00:35:19.170 --> 00:35:20.640
They have chronic conditions.

00:35:20.640 --> 00:35:22.050
Cancer, of course, is one of them,

00:35:22.050 --> 00:35:24.840
but I'm sure there's diabetes,
there's hypertension,

00:35:24.840 --> 00:35:27.633
there's all kinds of NCDs, but in general,

00:35:29.430 --> 00:35:32.340
humanitarian organizations
don't seem to focus

00:35:32.340 --> 00:35:34.260
as much on NCDs as they ought to.

00:35:34.260 --> 00:35:37.410
So I don't know if you have
any thoughts about that,

00:35:37.410 --> 00:35:39.600
how do we make that case

00:35:39.600 --> 00:35:43.350
that NCDs should become a priority

00:35:43.350 --> 00:35:45.330
for humanitarian organization?

00:35:45.330 --> 00:35:50.070
The other question I had was
on breast cancer prevention.

00:35:50.070 --> 00:35:54.630
I'm just wondering if
there's any strategies

00:35:54.630 --> 00:35:58.500
that can be done in reducing breast cancer

00:35:58.500 --> 00:36:02.163
in displaced populations
in humanitarian setting.

00:36:03.240 --> 00:36:08.240
- Yeah, so two very important questions.

00:36:08.520 --> 00:36:11.730
The first one is really difficult, right?

00:36:11.730 --> 00:36:14.610
Because we know,

00:36:14.610 --> 00:36:17.490
particularly for cancer,

00:36:17.490 --> 00:36:19.863
it's an expensive disease to treat.

00:36:20.850 --> 00:36:23.100
It is an expensive disease to treat

00:36:23.100 --> 00:36:27.630
because it requires a
multi-disciplinary effort.

00:36:27.630 --> 00:36:29.700
It requires a lot of infrastructure.

00:36:29.700 --> 00:36:31.293
It requires specialists.

00:36:32.910 --> 00:36:35.760
And so for humanitarian organizations

00:36:35.760 --> 00:36:39.420
who are trying to deliver care,

00:36:39.420 --> 00:36:43.230
one can imagine that when they look

00:36:43.230 --> 00:36:48.217
at their limited resources,
that they say to themselves,

00:36:48.217 --> 00:36:50.530
"Where's the best place to spend our buck

00:36:51.510 --> 00:36:56.430
and kind of get the biggest wins

00:36:56.430 --> 00:36:59.160
that we can in the shortest
period of time that we can

00:36:59.160 --> 00:37:01.287
for the least amount of money as we can?"

00:37:02.130 --> 00:37:06.600
And so I think that that, therefore,

00:37:06.600 --> 00:37:09.247
takes people into the route of,

00:37:09.247 --> 00:37:11.520
"Well, let's make sure
that there's clean water.

00:37:11.520 --> 00:37:13.650
Let's make sure that there's sanitation.

00:37:13.650 --> 00:37:16.893
Let's make sure that we can feed children.

00:37:17.760 --> 00:37:20.907
Let's make sure that we can
treat infectious diseases."

00:37:22.320 --> 00:37:25.683
And those things are
important, but to your point,

00:37:26.820 --> 00:37:30.813
as we see these conflicts
that are not limited in time,

00:37:31.860 --> 00:37:33.420
that are extensive,

00:37:33.420 --> 00:37:37.440
we know that the mortality associated

00:37:37.440 --> 00:37:42.440
with noncommunicable diseases
is gonna far overtake

00:37:43.230 --> 00:37:46.263
the mortality associated
with communicable diseases.

00:37:47.880 --> 00:37:51.960
But I think that the
case is difficult to make

00:37:51.960 --> 00:37:54.190
to these humanitarian organizations

00:37:55.500 --> 00:37:57.900
because it is a big lift, right?

00:37:57.900 --> 00:38:02.280
It is a lot of money to expend,

00:38:02.280 --> 00:38:05.670
and particularly for things like cancer,

00:38:05.670 --> 00:38:07.800
which is so complex,

00:38:07.800 --> 00:38:10.380
you cannot think about
breast cancer being the same

00:38:10.380 --> 00:38:12.330
as prostate cancer or lung cancer

00:38:12.330 --> 00:38:14.073
or colon cancer or leukemia.

00:38:15.030 --> 00:38:18.123
And so now you're treating
many different diseases.

00:38:20.880 --> 00:38:24.120
It becomes difficult
to have the expertise,

00:38:24.120 --> 00:38:25.980
the infrastructure, et cetera,

00:38:25.980 --> 00:38:29.250
whereas other noncommunicable diseases,

00:38:29.250 --> 00:38:33.930
heart disease, hypertension,
diabetes, might be easier.

00:38:33.930 --> 00:38:35.700
And I think the last point that I'll make

00:38:35.700 --> 00:38:38.280
is that the impact that you see,

00:38:38.280 --> 00:38:41.070
many of these humanitarian organizations,

00:38:41.070 --> 00:38:43.320
they want to see impact

00:38:43.320 --> 00:38:46.470
because that's how they,

00:38:46.470 --> 00:38:48.303
that's their life blood, right?

00:38:49.698 --> 00:38:52.740
They are relying on funders.

00:38:52.740 --> 00:38:56.493
They're relying on people,
philanthropic donations,

00:38:58.170 --> 00:38:59.640
to fund their mission.

00:38:59.640 --> 00:39:02.197
Part of the way that they
harness that is to say,

00:39:02.197 --> 00:39:05.400
"Look at the impact that we're having."

00:39:05.400 --> 00:39:09.390
And cancer is one of these where impact

00:39:09.390 --> 00:39:12.197
is measured not in days but in years,

00:39:12.197 --> 00:39:16.500
(chuckling) and it can take a
long time to see your impact.

00:39:16.500 --> 00:39:19.680
And so I think those are the issues

00:39:19.680 --> 00:39:22.533
that these humanitarian
organizations face.

00:39:23.520 --> 00:39:26.520
I think one of the ways
to get over that is

00:39:26.520 --> 00:39:28.950
to do a better job in terms

00:39:28.950 --> 00:39:33.750
of taking care of the immediate issues

00:39:33.750 --> 00:39:38.070
so that once those
immediate issues are done,

00:39:38.070 --> 00:39:40.687
then these humanitarian
organizations can kinda say,

00:39:40.687 --> 00:39:42.777
"Well, what else is there to do?"

00:39:43.921 --> 00:39:45.930
and then work at the next issue,

00:39:45.930 --> 00:39:49.350
which will be the
noncommunicable diseases.

00:39:49.350 --> 00:39:50.640
Now this is not to say

00:39:50.640 --> 00:39:53.460
that I don't think they
should do work in cancer,

00:39:53.460 --> 00:39:57.333
this is my life blood, but I understand,

00:39:58.200 --> 00:40:01.233
I understand the difficulty
that these NGOs face.

00:40:02.820 --> 00:40:04.590
In terms of prevention,

00:40:04.590 --> 00:40:07.290
this is another one that's hard, right?

00:40:07.290 --> 00:40:11.820
Because if we think
about primary prevention,

00:40:11.820 --> 00:40:14.280
which is really what I
think you're getting at,

00:40:14.280 --> 00:40:17.580
how do you prevent the
disease from the first place,

00:40:17.580 --> 00:40:18.840
it's really hard.

00:40:18.840 --> 00:40:21.240
We think about primary
prevention and breast cancer,

00:40:21.240 --> 00:40:23.220
reducing risk factors, well,

00:40:23.220 --> 00:40:24.720
what are the two main risk factors

00:40:24.720 --> 00:40:26.910
for developing breast cancer?

00:40:26.910 --> 00:40:31.383
Being born female at
birth and getting older.

00:40:32.310 --> 00:40:33.710
Like, what are you gonna do?

00:40:34.740 --> 00:40:36.723
Family history plays in.

00:40:37.590 --> 00:40:41.070
If we can avoid nuclear disasters,

00:40:41.070 --> 00:40:43.770
radiation plays into breast cancer,

00:40:43.770 --> 00:40:47.073
so if we can have world
peace, that might help.

00:40:49.110 --> 00:40:54.110
But the modifiable risk factors are few.

00:40:54.960 --> 00:40:57.240
And they're the ones that
everybody knows about, right?

00:40:57.240 --> 00:41:00.720
So if you can reduce obesity,

00:41:00.720 --> 00:41:05.670
increase physical activity,
maintain ideal body weight,

00:41:05.670 --> 00:41:08.820
we know that you will reduce
your risk of many cancers,

00:41:08.820 --> 00:41:11.850
and, P.S., you will also
reduce your risk of many NCDs

00:41:11.850 --> 00:41:15.030
that are also key killers,
right, heart disease, diabetes,

00:41:15.030 --> 00:41:16.480
stroke, et cetera, et cetera,

00:41:17.730 --> 00:41:22.290
but we also know that our world population

00:41:22.290 --> 00:41:25.410
is getting more and more obese.

00:41:25.410 --> 00:41:27.810
So if we could do something on that front,

00:41:27.810 --> 00:41:30.960
that potentially is a
very cost-effective way

00:41:30.960 --> 00:41:32.670
of having a meaningful impact.

00:41:32.670 --> 00:41:35.520
Will it have an impact that
will be measurable in days?

00:41:35.520 --> 00:41:38.070
No, but it may have an impact

00:41:38.070 --> 00:41:40.740
that will be measurable in years.

00:41:40.740 --> 00:41:43.050
Other things, alcohol.

00:41:43.050 --> 00:41:46.770
Well, okay, but
particularly in Middle East

00:41:46.770 --> 00:41:50.130
with a large Muslim population,

00:41:50.130 --> 00:41:53.400
that may not be as much of an issue

00:41:53.400 --> 00:41:57.153
as it might be in Western countries.

00:41:58.920 --> 00:42:02.430
Exogenous hormones, taking
hormone replacement therapy,

00:42:02.430 --> 00:42:05.050
again, something that in

00:42:07.230 --> 00:42:10.110
developing countries
may not be as prevalent

00:42:10.110 --> 00:42:12.030
as it is here in the West.

00:42:12.030 --> 00:42:15.210
Even here in the West,
we've seen that after

00:42:15.210 --> 00:42:19.680
the Women's Health Initiative
study came out demonstrating

00:42:19.680 --> 00:42:23.580
that exogenous hormones increase

00:42:23.580 --> 00:42:26.790
the risk of breast cancer,
we saw that rate decline

00:42:26.790 --> 00:42:31.203
and similarly saw a decline
in breast cancer instance.

00:42:32.884 --> 00:42:35.490
But if we think about as a whole,

00:42:35.490 --> 00:42:39.690
breast cancer is one of those
where there aren't a lot

00:42:39.690 --> 00:42:43.650
of things we can do in
terms of primary prevention.

00:42:43.650 --> 00:42:47.700
The other things is on
the family history side

00:42:47.700 --> 00:42:51.660
and the genetics, we've
done studies, for example,

00:42:51.660 --> 00:42:55.020
in Trinidad and Tobago, in Pakistan,

00:42:55.020 --> 00:42:56.910
where we found a high prevalence

00:42:56.910 --> 00:43:00.630
of triple negative breast
cancers in younger populations,

00:43:00.630 --> 00:43:03.450
trying to see whether
they had a higher rate

00:43:03.450 --> 00:43:05.430
of genetic mutations.

00:43:05.430 --> 00:43:07.110
If they have a genetic mutation,

00:43:07.110 --> 00:43:11.310
it doesn't mean that we now
magically can resolve this,

00:43:11.310 --> 00:43:13.530
but women can do things

00:43:13.530 --> 00:43:16.470
that will prophylactically
reduce their risk, right?

00:43:16.470 --> 00:43:19.020
So you can do prophylactic mastectomies,

00:43:19.020 --> 00:43:22.380
which will reduce your risk by 95%.

00:43:22.380 --> 00:43:25.110
However, then you come
into the other problem,

00:43:25.110 --> 00:43:28.350
which is in many of these areas,

00:43:28.350 --> 00:43:30.483
in the Middle East, in Asia,

00:43:32.160 --> 00:43:34.810
there are cultural stigmata

00:43:35.730 --> 00:43:38.727
that play into this, right?

00:43:38.727 --> 00:43:42.810
And so even patients who face
a diagnosis of breast cancer,

00:43:42.810 --> 00:43:46.237
many of them will say,

00:43:46.237 --> 00:43:49.380
"I was really scared to have treatment

00:43:49.380 --> 00:43:51.720
because if I lost my breasts,

00:43:51.720 --> 00:43:54.210
then I would not have been marriageable,

00:43:54.210 --> 00:43:56.310
and if I was not marriageable,

00:43:56.310 --> 00:44:00.603
then I would have been excluded
from the community," right?

00:44:01.560 --> 00:44:04.357
And so the aspect of,

00:44:04.357 --> 00:44:07.410
"Well, if we only knew the
genetics of the population,

00:44:07.410 --> 00:44:11.610
we could take steps to
prophylactically reduce risk,"

00:44:11.610 --> 00:44:15.300
also has problems, right?

00:44:15.300 --> 00:44:18.090
So this is one of the things
that I always find challenging

00:44:18.090 --> 00:44:22.170
about global health,
is that for everything

00:44:22.170 --> 00:44:25.590
that you think of as a potential solution,

00:44:25.590 --> 00:44:28.650
there are nine other problems that emerge.

00:44:28.650 --> 00:44:30.600
It's kind of like this onion

00:44:30.600 --> 00:44:33.810
that you keep peeling
the layers of the onion,

00:44:33.810 --> 00:44:36.600
and there's still more layers underneath.

00:44:36.600 --> 00:44:39.390
So Kaveh, you've had more experience

00:44:39.390 --> 00:44:41.130
than me in this whole area.

00:44:41.130 --> 00:44:42.483
What are your thoughts?

00:44:43.560 --> 00:44:48.150
- Well, I mean, I developed
my Humanitarian Research Lab

00:44:48.150 --> 00:44:50.880
because I want the
research to be beneficial

00:44:50.880 --> 00:44:54.993
to humanitarian organizations
and policymakers.

00:44:55.920 --> 00:44:58.770
I think that, to me, is a critical thing.

00:44:58.770 --> 00:45:00.990
I'm so glad that you've
done this research,

00:45:00.990 --> 00:45:04.560
and I'm really hopeful that SAMS is able

00:45:04.560 --> 00:45:08.580
to use these evidence

00:45:08.580 --> 00:45:13.290
and to apply for additional funds,

00:45:13.290 --> 00:45:15.540
to get more support,

00:45:15.540 --> 00:45:18.090
because now they have some data to share.

00:45:18.090 --> 00:45:20.433
So that's really,

00:45:22.290 --> 00:45:24.300
I feel like just bringing
some attention to this

00:45:24.300 --> 00:45:27.000
and doing research that
humanitarian organizations

00:45:27.000 --> 00:45:29.340
may not have the time or
the capacity to do that,

00:45:29.340 --> 00:45:31.653
that's our role as an
academic institution.

00:45:33.300 --> 00:45:37.920
- Yeah, and can I ask as well,

00:45:37.920 --> 00:45:41.640
because these are areas

00:45:41.640 --> 00:45:43.990
that are clearly from being affected

00:45:45.240 --> 00:45:47.760
vastly by the conflict.

00:45:47.760 --> 00:45:50.880
So how hard is for these institutions

00:45:50.880 --> 00:45:55.470
that are able to give the
support to continue doing that?

00:45:55.470 --> 00:45:57.780
Because, obviously, it's very different

00:45:57.780 --> 00:46:01.140
if you are in a peaceful setting

00:46:01.140 --> 00:46:03.510
and then to be able to plan ahead

00:46:03.510 --> 00:46:06.300
rather than just trying to survive.

00:46:06.300 --> 00:46:07.850
And then you were saying about,

00:46:08.730 --> 00:46:12.300
I mean, all of the
diseases that they look at,

00:46:12.300 --> 00:46:15.750
I mean, you would imagine
that probably trauma

00:46:15.750 --> 00:46:18.900
will be one of the very first one

00:46:18.900 --> 00:46:22.980
that they have to or they take care of,

00:46:22.980 --> 00:46:27.570
both physically and
hopefully mentally as well.

00:46:27.570 --> 00:46:29.407
- Yeah, yeah, no.

00:46:32.640 --> 00:46:34.330
I think that SAMS

00:46:36.270 --> 00:46:38.940
has been blessed

00:46:38.940 --> 00:46:43.860
to have a reasonable philanthropic arm

00:46:43.860 --> 00:46:46.980
that helps to fund some of its work,

00:46:46.980 --> 00:46:50.890
and they are very committed
to really improving

00:46:51.870 --> 00:46:56.133
particularly cancer care
in northwestern Syria.

00:46:57.060 --> 00:47:01.560
But to your point, I think that we saw

00:47:01.560 --> 00:47:05.040
that even SAMS facilities

00:47:05.040 --> 00:47:09.690
had been embroiled in the conflict, right?

00:47:09.690 --> 00:47:11.760
We know that the conflict had targeted

00:47:11.760 --> 00:47:16.470
healthcare facilities,
and so it's really tragic

00:47:16.470 --> 00:47:20.790
when you're trying to help populations,

00:47:20.790 --> 00:47:25.350
and you're finding yourself
on the receiving end

00:47:25.350 --> 00:47:28.263
of the conflict that
you're trying to help.

00:47:29.580 --> 00:47:32.190
- And to the part of the cost,

00:47:32.190 --> 00:47:34.650
they're able to provide all

00:47:34.650 --> 00:47:38.100
of these services without any-

00:47:38.100 --> 00:47:42.390
- Yeah, so they offer their
services free of charge,

00:47:42.390 --> 00:47:44.920
but, again, I think that
they are very reliant

00:47:46.230 --> 00:47:51.033
on donors and on
fundraising and on grants,

00:47:52.590 --> 00:47:56.130
but, so far, my understanding is

00:47:56.130 --> 00:47:58.863
that they do offer all of
this care free of charge.

00:48:02.460 --> 00:48:03.963
Angie, I see your hand.

00:48:05.220 --> 00:48:08.190
- [Angie] Hi, this is just terrific.

00:48:08.190 --> 00:48:09.420
Thank you so much for sharing

00:48:09.420 --> 00:48:11.253
and for doing such important work.

00:48:12.420 --> 00:48:15.363
I have a question more around methods,

00:48:16.320 --> 00:48:19.567
and I love how you have triangulated.

00:48:20.520 --> 00:48:23.280
You looked the epidemiology of it.

00:48:23.280 --> 00:48:24.630
You have thought of the possibilities

00:48:24.630 --> 00:48:26.130
of the molecular genetics of it,

00:48:26.130 --> 00:48:28.650
but you've also done some of
the qualitative work around it.

00:48:28.650 --> 00:48:30.720
So I love, I love the way

00:48:30.720 --> 00:48:33.510
that you are truly approaching this

00:48:33.510 --> 00:48:35.610
from mixed-method approach.

00:48:35.610 --> 00:48:37.350
Can you take a little bit about

00:48:37.350 --> 00:48:41.403
what the qualitative process looked like?

00:48:42.660 --> 00:48:46.890
Was this mostly looking
for emergent themes

00:48:46.890 --> 00:48:49.110
in the experiences of women

00:48:49.110 --> 00:48:51.810
or did you come in with a priori,

00:48:51.810 --> 00:48:53.550
maybes ideas or assumptions

00:48:53.550 --> 00:48:57.090
of what was highly relevant to them?

00:48:57.090 --> 00:48:59.520
And then the second part
to that question is,

00:48:59.520 --> 00:49:01.530
what does sort of

00:49:01.530 --> 00:49:04.710
the Syrian academic engagement look like?

00:49:04.710 --> 00:49:07.390
What potential, what role do you see there

00:49:08.550 --> 00:49:13.550
in kind of complex public
health questions like this one?

00:49:14.490 --> 00:49:15.440
Thank you.
- Yeah.

00:49:17.250 --> 00:49:21.720
So, full disclosure, I was
not directly participating

00:49:21.720 --> 00:49:24.060
in the qualitative portion.

00:49:24.060 --> 00:49:29.060
This was really done by our
colleagues through SAMS.

00:49:29.220 --> 00:49:33.999
My Arabic is much worse than their English

00:49:33.999 --> 00:49:38.999
(chuckles) 'cause my Arabic
is pretty much non-existent.

00:49:39.120 --> 00:49:40.980
But my understanding is

00:49:40.980 --> 00:49:45.190
that the interviews were conducted

00:49:46.380 --> 00:49:50.230
without any necessarily a priori

00:49:54.240 --> 00:49:57.150
understanding of themes that would emerge,

00:49:57.150 --> 00:50:00.330
but really those are themes that came out

00:50:00.330 --> 00:50:03.123
of the conversations as they occurred.

00:50:08.100 --> 00:50:11.310
The researchers who had much more

00:50:11.310 --> 00:50:14.820
than experiencing qualitative
research than I did

00:50:14.820 --> 00:50:19.270
kind of applied this framework methods

00:50:20.190 --> 00:50:23.520
to the interviews that they got,

00:50:23.520 --> 00:50:27.210
used NVivo to help code for themes,

00:50:27.210 --> 00:50:30.540
had consultation between investigators

00:50:30.540 --> 00:50:32.760
to really parse out what were the themes

00:50:32.760 --> 00:50:36.030
that were emerging and
kind of went from there.

00:50:36.030 --> 00:50:38.790
In terms of the second question,

00:50:38.790 --> 00:50:43.410
academic partnerships,

00:50:43.410 --> 00:50:46.863
I think that this is still emerging.

00:50:47.880 --> 00:50:49.980
So this was the first project

00:50:49.980 --> 00:50:53.880
that I had done with SAMS.

00:50:53.880 --> 00:50:56.260
They have researchers

00:50:57.240 --> 00:51:00.330
both located within Syria,

00:51:00.330 --> 00:51:02.790
at the university there,

00:51:02.790 --> 00:51:06.240
as well as in the UK and in the US.

00:51:06.240 --> 00:51:09.870
And so we were able to
kind of partner together

00:51:09.870 --> 00:51:13.740
in a transnational way to answer questions

00:51:13.740 --> 00:51:16.260
that were important to all parties.

00:51:16.260 --> 00:51:19.683
Kaveh, I don't know if
you wanna add anything.

00:51:21.510 --> 00:51:25.380
- I think you covered
what I was thinking about.

00:51:25.380 --> 00:51:28.680
I was also thinking about how we were,

00:51:28.680 --> 00:51:30.210
I know you were particularly interested

00:51:30.210 --> 00:51:33.930
in trying to hire Syrian medical students

00:51:33.930 --> 00:51:36.790
in northwest Syria to participate in this.

00:51:36.790 --> 00:51:40.020
There was a lot of policy issues

00:51:40.020 --> 00:51:44.730
around being able to
fund students in Syria,

00:51:44.730 --> 00:51:48.540
so that was a bit challenging,
but we somehow still managed.

00:51:48.540 --> 00:51:50.040
It took a couple of months to figure out

00:51:50.040 --> 00:51:55.040
how to do the qualitative work, but, yeah.

00:51:55.080 --> 00:51:58.710
- Yeah, and to that point, Angie,

00:51:58.710 --> 00:52:00.810
I think that Kaveh brings up a good point,

00:52:00.810 --> 00:52:04.740
is there are regulations

00:52:04.740 --> 00:52:08.700
between what can pass
between the US and Syria

00:52:08.700 --> 00:52:12.960
because of embargoes,
and so on, and so forth.

00:52:12.960 --> 00:52:14.553
We complied with that, right?

00:52:16.590 --> 00:52:20.523
There are rules that you have
to color within the lines,

00:52:21.730 --> 00:52:24.780
but hopefully you can still collaborate

00:52:24.780 --> 00:52:28.380
and find interesting
ways that you can partner

00:52:28.380 --> 00:52:30.093
to answer important questions.

00:52:30.930 --> 00:52:33.430
- [Angie] Wonderful, thank
you. Thank you so much.

00:52:34.470 --> 00:52:36.090
- Thank you, everybody, for joining us,

00:52:36.090 --> 00:52:37.620
especially Angie from Bhutan.

00:52:37.620 --> 00:52:38.850
So glad to see you.

00:52:38.850 --> 00:52:40.830
Angie has done some wonderful work

00:52:40.830 --> 00:52:43.860
on early child development in Lebanon

00:52:43.860 --> 00:52:47.790
and in many other countries,
so thanks for joining us.

00:52:47.790 --> 00:52:49.620
Dr. Chagpar, thank you so much.

00:52:49.620 --> 00:52:50.970
This was incredible,

00:52:50.970 --> 00:52:55.530
and I really hope that this
research will make a difference

00:52:55.530 --> 00:52:58.257
for the Syrian population who
are struggling with cancer

00:52:58.257 --> 00:53:01.393
and many other issues, so thank you.

00:53:01.393 --> 00:53:03.780
- All right, thanks, everyone. Take care.

00:53:03.780 --> 00:53:05.673
Have a great day.
- Later. Bye bye.

00:53:06.900 --> 00:53:10.470
- Thank you very much for
the presentation excellence.

